 enum class Style : uint8_t
 {
     Plate,  ///< a name above a body: label, optional distance, legibility backdrop
     Card,   ///< an interaction prompt: keycap, label, sublabel, hold progress
     Ring,   ///< a ground circle, as a per-point projected polyline
     Dot,    ///< a filled marker with an outline
+    /// A WebUI surface composited onto a world quad -- a television screen.
+    ///
+    /// This is the one style that is not a shape drawn from the atlas, and the
+    /// paragraph above about "something genuinely new belongs in a web page" is
+    /// not violated by it: the *content* here is a page, already rasterised by
+    /// CEF into a texture we hold a descriptor for. What the overlay supplies is
+    /// the one thing a page cannot supply for itself -- where on the *world* the
+    /// rectangle goes.
+    ///
+    /// The honest limitation is occlusion. The overlay pass has no depth buffer
+    /// (see the `Draw` comment), so a screen the producer decides is visible is
+    /// painted over whatever geometry stands between the camera and it. The
+    /// producer is expected to make that decision itself -- `Api::MediaScreens`
+    /// line-of-sight tests the screen's centre -- and the whole quad is then
+    /// either drawn or not. A partially occluded screen is therefore drawn whole.
+    Screen,
 };
 
 [[nodiscard]] const char* Describe(Style aStyle);
 
